#{["Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0$. For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fact that whenever we multiply a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix, the matrix remains unchanged.\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_3$, we find that the result is just $A$ itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA \\cdot I_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(-2) \\cdot 1 + 3 \\cdot 0 + 10 \\cdot 0 & (-2) \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot 1 + 10 \\cdot 0 & (-2) \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot 0 + 10 \\cdot 1 \\\\ 6 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 0 & 6 \\cdot 0 + (-2) \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 0 & 6 \\cdot 0 + (-2) \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\ 23 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 0 + 8 \\cdot 0 & 23 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 1 + 8 \\cdot 0 & 23 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 0 + 8 \\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA \\cdot I_3 = A\n$$\n\nYou can check that\n\n$$\nI_3 \\cdot A = A\n$$\n\nusing the same method.\n\nThis result holds in general:\n\n> For *any* square $n \\times n$ matrix $A$, we have\n> $A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A$.\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1$, namely that\n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\nfor any real number $a$." #edb/ref 13194139533439 #edb/ref 17592186045417 false] ["Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0$. For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fact that whenever we multiply a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix, the matrix remains unchanged.\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_3$, we find that the result is just $A$ itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA \\cdot I_{3} &= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}(-2) \\cdot 1 + 3 \\cdot 0 + 10 \\cdot 0 & (-2) \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot 1 + 10 \\cdot 0 & (-2) \\cdot 0 + 3 \\cdot 0 + 10 \\cdot 1 \\\\ 6 \\cdot 1 + (-2) \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 0 & 6 \\cdot 0 + (-2) \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 0 & 6 \\cdot 0 + (-2) \\cdot 0 + 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\ 23 \\cdot 1 + 4 \\cdot 0 + 8 \\cdot 0 & 23 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 1 + 8 \\cdot 0 & 23 \\cdot 0 + 4 \\cdot 0 + 8 \\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= A\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nA \\cdot I_3 = A\n$$\n\nYou can check that\n\n$$\nI_3 \\cdot A = A\n$$\n\nusing the same method.\n\nThis result holds in general:\n\n> For *any* square $n \\times n$ matrix $A$, we have\n> $A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A$.\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1$, namely that\n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\nfor any real number $a$." #edb/ref 13194139533356 #edb/ref 17592186045417 true] ["Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0.$ For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\nI_3 = \n\t\t\t\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fact that whenever we multiply a matrix by the corresponding identity matrix, the matrix remains unchanged.\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nMultiplying the matrix $A$ by the identity matrix $I_3,$ we find that the result is just $A$ itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cdot {I}_{3} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 1+10\\cdot 0 & (-2)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot 0+10\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 0 & 6\\cdot 0+(-2)\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 23\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 1+8\\cdot 0 & 23\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 0+8\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 3 & 10 \\\\ 6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 23 & 4 & 8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A \\cdot I_3 = A.$ You can check that $I_3 \\cdot A = A$ using the same method.\n\nThis result holds in general: \n\n> For *any* square $n \\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have\n> \n> \n> $$\n> A \\cdot I_n = I_n \\cdot A = A.\n>\n$$\n\nIn other words, the $n \\times n$ identity matrix $I_n$ has a similar property to the number $1,$ namely that \n\n$$\na\\cdot 1 = 1\\cdot a = a\n$$\n\n for any real number $a.$" #edb/ref 13194139541356 #edb/ref 17592186576411 true] ["Remember that the **identity matrix** $I_n$ is an $n \\times n$ square matrix with $1$'s on the diagonal, and where all other elements are $0.$ For example, the $3 \\times 3$ identity matrix $I_3$ is given below:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe identity matrix gets its name from the fac